  "response": "A court confronted with two competing accounts of how an injury came about proceeds in two stages: it asks first whether the defendant's conduct is causally connected to the harm at all, and then whether some later act displaces that connection. The \"but for\" test asks whether the plaintiff's injury would have occurred absent the defendant's conduct. The \"but for\" test requires the plaintiff to prove that the defendant's conduct was the sole cause of the injury. An inquiry satisfied by showing that the harm would not have come about but for what the defendant did, and a demand for proof that nothing else contributed to the harm, cannot both state the same threshold of causal responsibility; the one formulation excludes the other. The plaintiff bears the burden of proving causation by a preponderance of the evidence. The defendant bears the burden of disproving causation in an ordinary negligence action. Placing the risk of non-persuasion on the injured party and placing on the party sued the task of dispelling the causal link cannot both describe the ordinary negligence action; the allocation cannot run in both directions at once.\n\nForeseeability is the principal criterion for deciding whether an intervening act is superseding, and it is this criterion that has produced the settled rule that a foreseeable intervening act does not relieve the defendant of liability. Negligent medical treatment of an injury caused by the defendant does not ordinarily cut off the defendant's liability. An intervening criminal act by a third party supersedes the defendant's negligence as a matter of law in every case. Treating predictability as the touchstone of supersession cannot be squared with a categorical severance of liability whenever the intervening act happens to be criminal, however predictable that crime may have been. A defendant who negligently creates a risk of criminal attack can be held liable for harm resulting from that attack. Automatic supersession in every instance of third-party crime rules out any liability founded on having negligently exposed the plaintiff to precisely such a crime.\n\nWhere the proof supports the second account, the third party's act broke the chain of causation between the defendant's negligence and the plaintiff's injury; that is to say, the intervening act operated as a superseding cause that cut off the defendant's liability for the injury. Whereas the defendant who negligently invites a criminal attack answers in damages for its consequences, the defendant whose negligence is displaced by a superseding intervention answers for nothing at all.\n\nCausation is ordinarily a question of fact reserved for the jury, and it is for that reason that expert testimony is commonly admitted to establish the mechanism of the plaintiff's injury. Even though causation is thus the jury's province as a rule, a directed verdict on causation is appropriate when the evidence permits only one reasonable conclusion. Accordingly, where the record will bear only the defendant's conduct or only the intervening act as the operative cause of the harm, the court may resolve the question itself rather than submit it.",
